# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored and does NOT travel to
# Vercel — set every one of these in the Vercel dashboard as well.
#
# NOT EVERY SECRET BELONGS HERE. The code that talks to Google and sends the
# auth emails runs inside Convex, so its credentials live on the Convex
# deployment (`npx convex env set`), not in this file and not on Vercel:
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET, AUTH_RESEND_KEY, EMAIL_FROM,
#   EMAIL_POSTAL_ADDRESS, SITE_URL, JWT_PRIVATE_KEY, JWKS,
#   ANALYTICS_INGEST_SECRET
# `npx convex env list` shows them. A variable set in the wrong place fails at
# runtime with a message about it being unset, which is a confusing way to find
# out — so check both lists.

# ── Convex ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The database, the auth provider and the place the Google and Calendar calls
# run. `npx convex dev` writes both of these itself the first time.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=

# ── Google Cloud ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE OAuth client, TWO separate redirect URIs. Both must be registered on it;
# removing either to tidy up breaks the other half of the product.
#   Sign-in with Google — handled by Convex Auth, on the Convex deployment's
#     own origin:
#       https://<deployment>.convex.site/api/auth/callback/google
#   Calendar access — handled by us, scope calendar.events (write-with-attendees,
#     not read-only free/busy, because the guest is an attendee). Its redirect
#     URI is our own route and must be registered for EVERY origin:
#       http://localhost:3000/api/google/callback
#       https://<vercel-preview-domain>/api/google/callback
#       https://<production-domain>/api/google/callback
#     A missing one fails as redirect_uri_mismatch and nothing else.
#
# The app itself needs these only to BUILD the calendar consent URL. The
# exchange, the refresh and the revoke all happen inside Convex, so the same
# pair must also be set there.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Resend ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Until DKIM verifies, Resend delivers only to the account owner's own address.
# This key sends the booking and welcome emails from the Next.js side. Convex
# needs its own copy as AUTH_RESEND_KEY for the verification and reset emails,
# which it renders and sends itself — see convex/lib/emails.ts.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Both of the next two are OPTIONAL, and their defaults in src/lib/env.ts are
# already the real values. Setting either here or in the Vercel dashboard
# OVERRIDES the code — an old value left in a dashboard is how production keeps
# sending a retired address long after the repo stopped containing one.
#   EMAIL_FROM            defaults to  Meetrao <hello@meetrao.com>
#   EMAIL_POSTAL_ADDRESS  defaults to  the address in src/lib/contact.ts
# A full physical address — street, city, postcode, country. A city alone does
# not satisfy CAN-SPAM, and bulk filters read this footer too.
#
# EMAIL_POSTAL_ADDRESS must ALSO be set on the Convex deployment. The two auth
# emails are rendered there and have no code default to fall back on; they
# throw rather than send a footer with no address in it.
EMAIL_FROM=
EMAIL_POSTAL_ADDRESS=

# ── App ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
